// Rep Max questions: Religious Studies (AQA 8062MA), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"grs-001","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":1,"q":"Which word describes the Christian belief that God is one being in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit?","o":["Incarnation","Trinity","Atonement","Ascension"],"a":1,"x":"The Trinity is the belief in one God in three persons. Incarnation is God becoming human, atonement is making amends, and the Ascension is Jesus returning to heaven."}
{"id":"grs-002","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":2,"q":"Why do some people say that evil and suffering are a problem for belief in the Christian God?","o":["An all-loving, all-powerful God would be expected to stop suffering","Christians believe God cannot see what happens on Earth","The Bible never mentions evil or suffering","Christians believe God took no part in creation"],"a":0,"x":"The problem of evil asks how an all-loving, all-powerful God can allow suffering. Christians respond in different ways, for example by pointing to free will."}
{"id":"grs-003","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":3,"q":"Which belief about life after death is held by many Roman Catholics but rejected by most Protestant churches?","o":["Purgatory, a state of purification after death before heaven","Judgement by God after death","The resurrection of the body","Heaven as eternal life with God"],"a":0,"x":"Catholics teach that some souls are cleansed in purgatory before entering heaven. Most Protestants reject this, but both groups believe in judgement, resurrection and heaven."}
{"id":"grs-004","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":1,"q":"What do Christians mean by the Ascension?","o":["Jesus rising from the dead on the third day","Jesus returning to heaven forty days after the resurrection","Jesus being baptised by John in the River Jordan","The Holy Spirit coming to the disciples"],"a":1,"x":"Christians believe that after the resurrection Jesus was taken up into heaven in front of his disciples (Acts 1). The Spirit coming at Pentecost is a separate event."}
{"id":"grs-005","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":2,"q":"What does the word 'atonement' mean in Christian teaching?","o":["God taking human form in Jesus","A priest announcing forgiveness in confession","Jesus' death repairing the broken relationship between God and humans","Jesus rising from the dead at Easter"],"a":2,"x":"Atonement means 'at-one-ment': Christians believe Jesus' death on the cross made up for human sin and restored people's relationship with God. God becoming human is the incarnation."}
{"id":"grs-006","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best matches the Protestant (especially Lutheran) view of how people are saved?","o":["Salvation is earned only by keeping the Ten Commandments","Salvation comes only through sacraments given by a priest","Salvation is God's free gift of grace, received through faith in Christ","All people are saved whatever they believe or do"],"a":2,"x":"Many Protestants teach justification by faith: salvation is God's free gift of grace, not earned by good works. Catholics also teach that salvation comes by grace, but stress faith shown in good works and the sacraments."}
{"id":"grs-007","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":1,"q":"Which Christian sacrament uses water to welcome a person into the Church?","o":["Eucharist","Marriage","Baptism","Anointing of the sick"],"a":2,"x":"Baptism uses water, poured or by full immersion, to show cleansing from sin and entry to the Church. The Eucharist uses bread and wine; anointing uses oil."}
{"id":"grs-008","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"A church service that follows a set order of prayers and readings is called:","o":["Liturgical worship","Non-liturgical worship","Charismatic worship","Private worship"],"a":0,"x":"Liturgical worship follows a fixed pattern, as in Catholic and Anglican services. Non-liturgical worship is freer, and charismatic worship stresses the Holy Spirit."}
{"id":"grs-009","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"Many Catholics go on pilgrimage to Lourdes in France because they believe that:","o":["Jesus was crucified there","St Columba founded a monastery there in 563","the first Christian church was built there","Mary appeared to a girl called Bernadette there in 1858"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics believe Mary appeared to Bernadette Soubirous at Lourdes in 1858. Many pilgrims seek healing from its spring. Iona in Scotland is linked to St Columba."}
{"id":"grs-010","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a Christian charity that works to end poverty around the world?","o":["Greenpeace","Amnesty International","Christian Aid","Médecins Sans Frontières"],"a":2,"x":"Christian Aid is supported by many UK churches and works to tackle poverty overseas. Greenpeace, Amnesty and MSF are not Christian organisations."}
{"id":"grs-011","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"What is evangelism?","o":["Helping refugees to settle in a new country","Praying quietly in a church building","Sharing the Christian message in order to bring others to faith","Making peace between two groups in conflict"],"a":2,"x":"Evangelism means telling others about Jesus and inviting them to become Christians. Helping the poor or making peace are different parts of the Church's work."}
{"id":"grs-012","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":3,"q":"Why do many Christians quote the parable of the sheep and the goats (Matthew 25) when supporting work to end poverty?","o":["Jesus says the poor will always be with us, so nothing can change","Jesus teaches that wealth is a sign of God's blessing","Jesus says charity should always be kept secret","Jesus says that helping people in need is the same as helping him"],"a":3,"x":"In Matthew 25 Jesus says that what people did for 'the least of these' they did for him. Christians use it to argue that they must feed and care for the poor."}
{"id":"grs-013","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":1,"q":"What does the Muslim belief in Tawhid mean?","o":["The prophethood of Muhammad","The oneness of Allah","The Day of Judgement","The existence of angels"],"a":1,"x":"Tawhid is the belief that Allah is one, with no partners or equals. It is the most important Muslim belief and the heart of the Shahadah."}
{"id":"grs-014","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":2,"q":"Which statement best describes the belief in al-Qadr (predestination) held by many Muslims?","o":["Humans have no choice because every action is forced","Allah knows all that will happen, yet humans are free and accountable","Allah only learns what happens after it has happened","Allah decides who goes to heaven so actions do not matter"],"a":1,"x":"Many Muslims believe Allah's knowledge covers everything, but people still have free will and will be judged on their actions. Shi'a Muslims especially stress Allah's justice and human freedom."}
{"id":"grs-015","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":3,"q":"Which of these is one of the five Roots of Religion (Usul ad-Din) in Shi'a Islam?","o":["Al-Qadr, predestination","Adalat, the justice of Allah","Al-Malaikah, belief in angels","Salah, daily prayer"],"a":1,"x":"The Shi'a Roots are Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah and Ma'ad. Predestination and angels belong to the Sunni articles of faith; Salah is a practice, not a Root."}
{"id":"grs-016","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Muslims believe the Qur'an was revealed to Muhammad through which angel?","o":["Mika'il","Israfil","Azra'il","Jibril"],"a":3,"x":"Muslims believe Jibril (Gabriel) brought Allah's words to Muhammad over about 23 years. Mika'il, Israfil and Azra'il are other angels with different roles."}
{"id":"grs-017","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Which holy book do Muslims believe was given to the prophet Isa (Jesus)?","o":["Injil","Tawrat","Zabur","Sahifah"],"a":0,"x":"Muslims believe Allah sent earlier books: Tawrat to Musa, Zabur to Dawud and Injil to Isa. Muslims believe the Qur'an is Allah's final, unaltered revelation."}
{"id":"grs-018","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":3,"q":"What do Shi'a Muslims believe about the Imams?","o":["They are elected by the Muslim community after each leader dies","They receive new revelation and write new holy books","They are chosen by Allah, starting with Ali, to guide Muslims after Muhammad","They are prophets who come after Muhammad"],"a":2,"x":"Shi'a Muslims believe Allah appointed Ali and his descendants as Imams to guide Muslims. Sunni Muslims have caliphs chosen by the community, and both groups say Muhammad was the last prophet."}
{"id":"grs-019","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":1,"q":"Why do Muslims perform wudu before Salah?","o":["To mark the end of a fast","To make themselves ritually clean before praying","To show they can afford the Hajj","To prepare halal food"],"a":1,"x":"Wudu is the washing of hands, mouth, face, arms and feet before Salah. It makes a Muslim clean in body and mind to stand before Allah."}
{"id":"grs-020","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is one of the Shi'a Ten Obligatory Acts but is not one of the Five Pillars?","o":["Hajj","Sawm","Zakah","Khums"],"a":3,"x":"Khums is a tax of one fifth on surplus income, paid by Shi'a Muslims. Hajj, Sawm and Zakah are among the Five Pillars and also among the Ten Obligatory Acts."}
{"id":"grs-021","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":3,"q":"How does the Shahadah often differ among Shi'a Muslims compared with Sunni Muslims?","o":["Many Shi'a add a statement that Ali is the friend of Allah","They leave out the name of Muhammad","They say it only once in their lifetime","They replace it with the call to Salah"],"a":0,"x":"The Sunni Shahadah has two parts: no god but Allah, and Muhammad is his messenger. Many Shi'a add a third statement about Ali as the friend (wali) of Allah."}
{"id":"grs-022","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":1,"q":"In which month do Muslims fast from dawn to sunset for Sawm?","o":["Ramadan","Muharram","Shawwal","Dhul Hijjah"],"a":0,"x":"Sawm takes place in Ramadan, the ninth month, when the Qur'an was first revealed. Shawwal begins with Id-ul-Fitr, and Hajj takes place in Dhul Hijjah."}
{"id":"grs-023","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"What do Shi'a Muslims remember on Ashura?","o":["Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son","The martyrdom of Husayn at Karbala","The first revelation of the Qur'an to Muhammad","The end of the month of fasting"],"a":1,"x":"Ashura marks the death of Husayn, grandson of Muhammad, at Karbala in 680 CE. Many Sunni Muslims also fast on Ashura, remembering Allah saving Musa. Id-ul-Adha remembers Ibrahim, and Id-ul-Fitr ends Ramadan."}
{"id":"grs-024","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"On Hajj, what is the name for walking seven times around the Ka'bah?","o":["Sa'i","Wuquf","Ramy","Tawaf"],"a":3,"x":"Tawaf is circling the Ka'bah seven times. Sa'i is walking between Safa and Marwah, wuquf is standing at Arafat, and ramy is throwing stones at the pillars."}
{"id":"grs-025","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":1,"q":"Why does the Roman Catholic Church teach that marriage cannot normally be ended by divorce?","o":["Jesus never spoke about marriage","It is only a legal contract with no religious meaning","Divorce is allowed but only for men","Marriage is a sacrament, a lifelong covenant made before God"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics believe marriage is a sacrament that lasts for life. The Church does not recognise divorce, though it can grant an annulment, which says a valid marriage never existed."}
{"id":"grs-026","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"What is the usual Muslim teaching about divorce?","o":["It is forbidden in all circumstances","It is encouraged whenever a couple argue","It is allowed as a last resort but strongly discouraged","It is allowed only for men, who need no reason"],"a":2,"x":"In Islam marriage is a contract and divorce is permitted, for example by talaq or khula, but Muslims are urged to try reconciliation first. Both husbands and wives have ways to end a marriage."}
{"id":"grs-027","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"Which would a humanist be most likely to use to judge whether a sexual relationship is right?","o":["Whether a priest has blessed it","Whether it is likely to produce children","Whether it follows the Ten Commandments","Whether it is consensual and harms no one"],"a":3,"x":"Humanists base morality on reason, human welfare and consent rather than on religious rules. Many therefore accept sex outside marriage if all involved agree and nobody is harmed."}
{"id":"grs-028","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":1,"q":"In Christian teaching, which is a main purpose of the family?","o":["To bring up children to know and love God","To give fathers power over everyone else","To make sure children inherit money","To keep members apart from other communities"],"a":0,"x":"Christians see the family as the place where children are raised in love and taught the faith, and as a source of stability for society."}
{"id":"grs-029","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":2,"q":"Which Bible teaching do many Christians use to support equality between men and women?","o":["Wives should submit to their husbands (Ephesians 5)","Women should keep silent in the churches (1 Corinthians 14)","God created humans in his image, male and female (Genesis 1:27)","Adam was formed first, then Eve (1 Timothy 2)"],"a":2,"x":"Genesis 1:27 says both men and women are made in God's image. Some Christians use the other passages to argue for different roles, though others read them differently."}
{"id":"grs-030","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement reflects a common Muslim view of men and women?","o":["Women may not own property or earn money","Equal in worth before Allah, though many see family roles as different","Men and women must have identical roles in the family","Only men are responsible to Allah for their actions"],"a":1,"x":"Muslims teach that men and women are equal before Allah. Many see roles as complementary, with the husband providing, but Islam gives women rights to own property and to earn."}
{"id":"grs-031","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":1,"q":"Which scientific theory says the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago from a tiny, hot, dense point?","o":["Evolution by natural selection","Plate tectonics","The Big Bang theory","The Steady State theory"],"a":2,"x":"The Big Bang theory says the universe expanded from an extremely hot, dense state. Evolution explains how living things change, and Steady State is an older rival theory."}
{"id":"grs-032","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"What does the Muslim idea of khalifah mean for how Muslims treat the environment?","o":["Humans own the Earth and may use it however they like","Nature does not matter because only the afterlife matters","Humans are Allah's trustees on Earth and must look after it","Only animals, not plants, must be protected"],"a":2,"x":"A khalifah is a steward or trustee. Muslims believe Allah created the world and made humans responsible for caring for it, and they will answer to Allah for how they treated it."}
{"id":"grs-033","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"Christians who understand 'dominion' (Genesis 1:28) as stewardship would most likely say that humans should:","o":["Use animals and nature in any way that benefits humans","Care for creation on God's behalf and use resources responsibly","Never use animals or land for any purpose","Leave nature alone because God will look after it"],"a":1,"x":"Stewardship means looking after creation as God's trustees. Some Christians read dominion as the right to rule over nature, but many stress responsibility and care."}
{"id":"grs-034","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":1,"q":"What does the 'sanctity of life' mean?","o":["Life is only valuable if it is of good quality","Life is holy and is a gift from God","Life belongs to the person who lives it","Life has no purpose beyond the present"],"a":1,"x":"The sanctity of life is the religious belief that human life is sacred because God created it. It is often used to argue against abortion and euthanasia."}
{"id":"grs-035","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about Christian views on abortion is accurate?","o":["Roman Catholic: acceptable if the mother chooses it","Roman Catholic: allowed freely in the first 12 weeks","Church of England: always a sin, with no exceptions","Church of England: usually wrong, but may be a lesser evil in some cases"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics teach that life begins at conception and deliberate abortion is always wrong. The Church of England strongly opposes abortion but accepts that in strictly limited cases it may be morally preferable to any other choice."}
{"id":"grs-036","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":2,"q":"Which is a typical humanist argument in support of voluntary euthanasia?","o":["People should be free to choose a dignified death to avoid unbearable suffering","God alone has the right to end life","Suffering always makes a person stronger","Life must be preserved at all costs"],"a":0,"x":"Humanists do not believe in a God who controls life and death. Many stress quality of life and personal choice, with safeguards, while religious believers often stress the sanctity of life."}
{"id":"grs-037","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":1,"q":"What do pacifists believe?","o":["War is justified only if a religious leader declares it","Violence is acceptable if there is a just cause","War and violence are always wrong and disputes should be settled peacefully","Terrorism can be justified in extreme cases"],"a":2,"x":"Pacifists reject all war and violence. Just war theory allows war in some conditions, and holy war is fought for a religious cause. Quakers are well-known Christian pacifists."}
{"id":"grs-038","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is one of the conditions of a just war in Christian teaching?","o":["It must be a last resort after peaceful options have failed","It must be fought to gain land and resources","It must punish the enemy as harshly as possible","It must destroy the enemy's whole population"],"a":0,"x":"Just war conditions include just cause, right authority, right intention, last resort, proportionality and a reasonable chance of success. Greed or revenge are not just causes."}
{"id":"grs-039","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":3,"q":"Which statement describes the Muslim idea of 'lesser jihad'?","o":["The inner struggle to resist temptation and be a better Muslim","A pilgrimage to Makkah in the month of Dhul Hijjah","Armed struggle in defence of Islam or against oppression, with proper authority","Any war fought to spread Islam by force"],"a":2,"x":"Lesser jihad is armed struggle with strict conditions, such as defence of faith or fighting oppression. The inner struggle is the greater jihad. Many Muslims reject the idea that it means forcing belief."}
{"id":"grs-040","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":1,"q":"What does 'nuclear deterrence' mean?","o":["Getting rid of all nuclear weapons","Using nuclear weapons first to win quickly","Agreeing not to test nuclear weapons","Having nuclear weapons to discourage other countries from attacking"],"a":3,"x":"Deterrence is the idea that fear of massive retaliation stops enemies attacking. Getting rid of nuclear weapons is called disarmament."}
{"id":"grs-041","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"Which Archbishop chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, set up after apartheid?","o":["Oscar Romero","Justin Welby","Michael Ramsey","Desmond Tutu"],"a":3,"x":"Desmond Tutu, a Christian archbishop, chaired the commission set up in 1995. It encouraged people to tell the truth about abuses and to seek forgiveness and reconciliation."}
{"id":"grs-042","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"Why do many Christians say nuclear weapons could never meet the just war conditions?","o":["They would kill huge numbers of civilians, which is not proportionate","They are too cheap to be a last resort","They can only be used by a legitimate government","They are not mentioned in the Bible so are banned"],"a":0,"x":"Just war theory requires proportionality and protection of civilians. Christians who oppose nuclear weapons argue that their effects are indiscriminate and far out of proportion."}
{"id":"grs-043","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":1,"q":"What is a hate crime?","o":["Any crime committed in anger","A crime motivated by prejudice against a person's race, religion or similar","A crime committed by a group of people","A crime committed against a religious leader"],"a":1,"x":"A hate crime is committed because of hostility towards a person's race, religion, disability, sexual orientation or similar. Christians and Muslims believe all people are created by God, so all deserve respect."}
{"id":"grs-044","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"Which teaching do Christians often use to support obeying the law?","o":["Romans 13: be subject to the governing authorities","Matthew 5: blessed are the peacemakers","Genesis 1: humans are made in God's image","Luke 15: the parable of the lost sheep"],"a":0,"x":"Romans 13 says governing authorities have been set up by God, so Christians should obey them. Some Christians, such as Martin Luther King, say unjust laws may be broken."}
{"id":"grs-045","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"A Muslim teaching on murder, often quoted from Surah 5:32, says that killing one innocent person is like:","o":["Missing one of the daily prayers","Breaking a promise to Allah","Stealing from a neighbour","Killing the whole of humanity"],"a":3,"x":"Surah 5:32 says whoever kills an innocent life is as if he had killed all people. Muslims use it to show the great value Islam places on human life."}
{"id":"grs-046","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":1,"q":"Which aim of punishment is about putting people off committing crime through fear of the consequences?","o":["Reformation","Deterrence","Retribution","Protection"],"a":1,"x":"Deterrence aims to stop offenders and others from committing crime. Reformation aims to change offenders, retribution is getting 'payback', and protection keeps society safe."}
{"id":"grs-047","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":2,"q":"Which Quaker is remembered for campaigning to improve conditions for women prisoners in the 1800s?","o":["Josephine Butler","Florence Nightingale","Mary Seacole","Elizabeth Fry"],"a":3,"x":"Elizabeth Fry, a Quaker, visited Newgate prison and worked to improve conditions for women. Her work is an example of Christians trying to reform offenders."}
{"id":"grs-048","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":3,"q":"What is the Roman Catholic Church's current teaching on the death penalty?","o":["It is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person","It is required for all murder","It is acceptable whenever the state decides","It is allowed only for theft"],"a":0,"x":"In 2018 Pope Francis changed the Catechism to say the death penalty is inadmissible. Some other Christians and Muslims accept it for the most serious crimes."}
